    <!-- ===== Per-brand bullet-formatted tips ===== -->
    <!-- Each tip body uses "  •  " (NBSP + bullet + NBSP) at line starts so
         Compose Text wraps each bullet line correctly. Separated by \n\n for
         paragraph spacing. KEEP-AWAKE and VOICE sections are emitted as two
         strings each so the HelpScreen renders them with subsection
         headers between them. -->

    <string tools:ignore="MissingTranslation" name="setup_oem_keep_pixel">Stock Android has no extra OEM kill on top of standard Doze. If alarms still misbehave, check Settings → Notifications → Do not disturb → Allowed during DND → Alarms and sounds is ON.</string>
    <string tools:ignore="MissingTranslation" name="setup_oem_voice_pixel">Pixel ships with Google Clock as the default alarm app.\n\n  •  Say \"Hey Google, set an alarm\". Android may show a chooser if both apps qualify — pick GT Alarm and tap Always.\n  •  To swap later: Settings → Apps → Google Clock → Open by default → Clear defaults. Voice-trigger again and pick GT Alarm.</string>

    <string tools:ignore="MissingTranslation" name="setup_oem_keep_samsung">One UI puts unused apps to sleep aggressively. Walk through each item — at least one of them is usually the culprit.\n\n  •  Settings → Battery → Background usage limits → Never sleeping apps → + → add GT Alarm.\n  •  Confirm GT Alarm is NOT under Sleeping apps OR Deep sleeping apps in that screen.\n  •  Settings → Battery → ⋮ (top right) → Automation → Auto-optimize daily → OFF.\n  •  Settings → Battery → More battery settings → Adaptive battery → OFF.\n  •  For the paired Huawei watch, also add Huawei Health to Never sleeping apps.\n  •  Settings → Notifications → Do not disturb → Allowed during DND → Alarms and sounds → ON.</string>
    <string tools:ignore="MissingTranslation" name="setup_oem_voice_samsung">One UI does not expose a single \"default alarm app\" setting. Instead:\n\n  •  Say \"Hey Google, set an alarm\". The first time Google Assistant fires it, Android shows a chooser if both apps qualify — pick GT Alarm and tap Always.\n  •  Bixby may not honor third-party clocks. Stick with Google Assistant for voice.</string>

    <string tools:ignore="MissingTranslation" name="setup_oem_keep_xiaomi">MIUI / HyperOS has the most aggressive background kill on the market. Every step matters.\n\n  •  Settings → Apps → Manage apps → GT Alarm → Autostart → ON.\n  •  Same screen → Battery saver → No restrictions.\n  •  Same screen → Other permissions → Display pop-up windows while running in the background → ON.\n  •  Lock the app in the recents drawer (pull down the card → padlock icon) so MIUI does not reap it.</string>
    <string tools:ignore="MissingTranslation" name="setup_oem_voice_xiaomi">MIUI Clock is the system default.\n\n  •  Voice alarms via Google Assistant trigger a chooser the first time — pick GT Alarm + Always.\n  •  Third-party alarms may not show the alarm-clock icon in the status bar. This is a HyperOS limitation; the alarm still fires.</string>

    <string tools:ignore="MissingTranslation" name="setup_oem_keep_oneplus">OxygenOS deep-sleep can defer alarms by hours unless GT Alarm is exempted.\n\n  •  Settings → Battery → Battery optimization → GT Alarm → Don\'t optimize.\n  •  Settings → Apps → GT Alarm → Battery usage → Allow background activity → ON.\n  •  If your model has Advanced Optimization, exclude GT Alarm there too.</string>
    <string tools:ignore="MissingTranslation" name="setup_oem_voice_oneplus">OxygenOS uses OnePlus Clock by default.\n\n  •  First \"Hey Google, set an alarm\" should show a chooser — pick GT Alarm + tap Always.</string>

    <string tools:ignore="MissingTranslation" name="setup_oem_keep_huawei">EMUI / HarmonyOS NEXT requires explicit protection.\n\n  •  Phone Manager → Protected apps → enable GT Alarm.\n  •  Settings → Battery → Launch → GT Alarm → turn OFF \"Manage automatically\" then turn ON all three sub-toggles (Auto-launch, Secondary launch, Run in background).</string>
    <string tools:ignore="MissingTranslation" name="setup_oem_voice_huawei">Huawei phones without Google Mobile Services (most models since 2020) don\'t run Google Assistant, so voice-set isn\'t available via the GT Alarm intent. Native assistants (Celia, Bixby) typically don\'t honor third-party alarm apps.\n\nIf you have an older Huawei with GMS, follow the Pixel / stock Android steps.</string>

    <string tools:ignore="MissingTranslation" name="setup_oem_keep_other">If your phone is a Chinese brand (vivo, OPPO, Realme, Meizu) look for a Phone Manager or Security app and find a Protected apps / Autostart / Background app list. Add GT Alarm there.\n\nOn other brands the universal reliability checklist above is usually enough.</string>
    <string tools:ignore="MissingTranslation" name="setup_oem_voice_other">Most Android phones use the system Clock app by default.\n\n  •  When Google Assistant fires \"set an alarm\", the system shows a chooser if multiple apps qualify — pick GT Alarm and tap Always. The system remembers the choice per-intent.</string>
</resources>
